      "id": 1,
      "text": "Woodburn was first platted in 1865 by Joseph Edgerton and Joseph Smith, originally called Phelps Station because of its train depot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        5,
        6,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.woodburn.in.gov/1235/About-Woodburn-Indiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name changed to Woodburn in September 1865 after massive wood burnings cleared the land for farming.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodburn%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early commerce in Woodburn centered around a grain elevator and a train station that served the surrounding farms.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodburn%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Woodburn is often called the Crossroads of America, blending historic roots with a forward‑looking community spirit.",
